{
  "id": "physics-007",
  "title": "How does a rainbow form?",
  "category": "physics",
  "subject_area": "light and water droplets",
  "difficulty_level": "introductory",
  "target_audience": [
    "general public",
    "primary school",
    "secondary school"
  ],
  "central_question": "How can sunlight and rain make a band of colours in the sky?",
  "template": "principle",
  "principle_statement": "A rainbow forms when sunlight enters raindrops, bends, reflects off the back of each drop and bends again as it leaves, which spreads the light into its colours.",
  "domain_of_validity": "Sunlight shining on water droplets in the air, seen with the Sun behind the viewer.",
  "scientific_explanation": {
    "level_1": "A rainbow is made when sunlight shines through raindrops. The light bends as it goes into each drop, bounces off the back, and bends again on the way out. Different colours bend by different amounts, so white sunlight is spread out into a band of colours. You see a rainbow when the Sun is behind you and there are raindrops in front of you.",
    "level_2": "Sunlight is made of many colours, or wavelengths. When it enters a water droplet it slows down and bends, because water is denser than air. Violet, the shortest visible wavelength, bends the most, and red, the longest, bends the least. The light reflects once off the inside of the drop and bends again as it leaves, so the colours leave in slightly different directions. This puts red on top and violet on the bottom of the main rainbow. A fainter second rainbow comes from two reflections inside the drops, and its colours are in reverse order.",
    "level_3": "Refraction is the bending of light when it changes speed between air and water. Because the amount of bending depends on wavelength, white light is split into colours. For the primary rainbow, with one internal reflection, red light leaves at about 42 degrees. For the secondary rainbow, with two internal reflections, red light leaves at about 50 degrees, so the secondary bow appears above the primary one. For light, the speed equals the frequency times the wavelength, c = f L, so violet light, with the shortest wavelength, has the highest frequency. A full rainbow is a circle, but from the ground we see only the part above the horizon."
  },
  "supporting_evidence": [
    "Sunlight spread out by a glass prism shows the same band of colours as a rainbow.",
    "From an airplane, in the right conditions, a rainbow can be seen as a complete circle."
  ],
  "sources": [
